From cb6aeb3bea88ee18231efe83f17f77141bd7e959 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Hotchkiss <chris@hotchkiss.io>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 18:10:22 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] O4: oracle frame-locked input on pads 0 and 1 over the P1
 socket (GUAC_ORACLE=1)

Three verbs on P1's AF_UNIX listener so the oracle can walk, fight and play co-op flip by flip (openspec AF.7, design decision 3). Behind O3's gate: each answers P1's ERR unknown_cmd unless GUAC_ORACLE=1 is in the emulator's environment. Nothing knows the game: every flip is RSX's int_flip_index, the counter STATE and HB report.

- PAD <port> <flip> <d1> <d2> <lx> <ly> <rx> <ry> -> OK | ERR bad_args | ERR bad_port | ERR bad_flip | ERR bad_button | ERR bad_axis | ERR no_renderer | ERR past | ERR full. From <flip> on, port 0 or 1 reads CELL_PAD_CTRL_* words d1 and d2 (hex, 0-ff: the PS button is refused) and the four stick axes (decimal 0-255, 128 centred), held until that port's next entry; one at an existing flip replaces it. An entry has to be ahead of the counter (the current flip's reads may already have happened), so a schedule is loaded at a pause; 65,536 entries a port.
- PAD_CLEAR -> OK | ERR bad_args. Both schedules dropped; the pads read the host's input, and P6's presses, again.
- PAD_STATE <port> [<flip>] -> OK flip=<n> reads=<k> differ=<m> d1=<hex> d2=<hex> lx= ly= rx= ry= | ERR bad_args | ERR bad_port | ERR bad_flip | ERR no_read. Without a flip, the guest's last read on that port and the flip it fell in; with one, that flip's first read. reads counts the guest's reads in that flip and differ those that weren't the first one's state. The last 4,096 flips are kept a port.

Where it applies: cellPad's pad_get_data, under pad::g_pad_mutex (cellPadGetData and cellPadPeriphGetData hold it; the verbs take it). The read takes the counter once and, when a port's schedule has an entry at or before it, replaces what the host's buttons and sticks did to the pad's buffer with that entry, P6's forced presses included, running pad_get_data's own change check from the buffer as it was, so a report goes out only when the state changes. Pressed buttons read full pressure, as P6's do. Holding the mutex while checking the counter makes the past check exact: no read of a flip still ahead of it can have happened. A new emulation session (Emu.GetEmulationIdentifier(), so a boot or a savestate's reboot) drops the schedules and the reads, since its flips count from 0 again. Sensors, the PS button and pressure below full are left to the host.

Touches cellPad.cpp (the schedule, the readback, two spots in pad_get_data) and Skylander.cpp beside O3.

